statement.p:
 Give a description of the solution space to the
	  linear system:

	    \begin{alignedat}{3}
	    x \amp = \amp 2 \\
	    y \amp = \amp -1. \\
	    \end{alignedat}

 Give a description of the solution space to the
	  linear system:

	    \begin{alignedat}{4}
	    -x \amp {} + {} \amp 2y \amp {}-{} \amp z \amp {}={}
	    \amp -3 \\
	    \amp \amp 3y \amp {}+{} \amp z \amp {}={} \amp -1 \\
	    \amp \amp \amp \amp 2z \amp {}={} \amp 4. \\
	    \end{alignedat}

solution.p:
We first use the third equation to determine that z=2. We next substitute this value into the first two equations to obtain
\begin{alignedat}{3} -x \amp {} + {} \amp 2y \amp {}={} \amp -1 \\ \amp \amp 3y \amp {}={} \amp -3. \\ \end{alignedat}
Now we can use the second equation to determine that y=-1, substitute that value into the first equation, and determine that x=-1. This tells us that the linear system has one solution (x,y,z) = (-1,-1,2).

statement.p:
 Give a description of the solution space to the
	  linear system:

	    \begin{alignedat}{3}
	    x \amp {} + {} \amp 3y \amp {}={} \amp -1 \\
	    2x\amp {}+{} \amp y \amp {}={} \amp 3. \\
	    \end{alignedat}
